[P1] FR16-01 - Repair of a footbridge in a wetland A municipality decided to repair a wooden footbridge through a small wetland frequented by walkers and science classes. The 42-meter-long structure had several cracked blades and two unstable guardrails, but its main structure remained intact. Before any intervention, the technical team has closed the access and set up a temporary route. The work was to be short in order to limit the disturbance of the breeding birds seen near the pond in early spring Choice of repairs The diagnosis distinguished between the elements which were actually degraded and those which could be retained. Seventeen blades and four vertical supports were replaced. The new wood has been selected in a naturally durable fuel and delivered without aggressive surface treatment. The corroded fasteners were removed one by one to avoid damaging the adjacent parts. During the work, tools and falling material were placed on a tarp, since no screws or metal fragments were to remain in the damp vegetation. Particular attention was paid to the geometry of the plating The spacing between the blades was kept at about 8 mm to allow the water to flow while remaining comfortable for small wheels and rods. The height of the guardrail was checked along the entire length, and a slight misalignment was corrected near the north entrance. After 18 mm of rain, the team returned to check the areas where water could accumulate The bridge reopened on 6 May 2026 after a joint inspection by the technical service and the natural site manager The final report was not limited to a list of replaced parts: it also indicated the areas to be monitored during the annual visits. This approach extended the life of the structure without turning a local repair into a complete reconstruction. The department also kept photographs taken before and after the work, with the position of the replaced parts. These images will be used at the next inspection to distinguish a new deterioration from a defect already known and considered stable during the annual monitoring.
