[P1] FR16-03 - Testing of a radio relay on a mountain trail Course Method An amateur radio club prepared a coverage test along a mountain trail used for local sporting events. The aim was to see if a small temporary relay could improve communications between the car park, a shelter and two points behind a ridge. The team was not looking for maximum range; they wanted to identify where the link became unstable with ordinary portable antennas. The relay was installed on a support 2.4 m above the ground, away from electrical cables and heavy metal surfaces. Four pairs followed defined routes and made a call every 15 minutes. Each call had an ID, time, location, and a known control phrase. The correspondent at the shelter noted the readability on a simple scale and asked for a repeat if words were missing. At 10:45 a.m., a pairing got a proper communication at a location that was almost unusable without a relay. However, a lower wooded area remained difficult despite the general improvement. The technicians then slightly changed the orientation of the relay antenna and repeated two routes. The change was not presented as a scientific optimisation, as the number of measures was limited and conditions varied with the movement of people. It nevertheless showed that a stable and well-cleared installation was as important as the rated power The battery, given for 12 V, remained above 11.7 V throughout the test period The report concluded that a temporary relay could help with the organisation of an event, but that it did not remove shady areas. The authorities have therefore maintained mandatory crossing points and an independent rescue procedure. The trial provided a practical map of reliable and uncertain sectors, useful for preparing future teams without promising perfect coverage. The audio files of some calls were kept with the time and measurement point. They will enable new operators to understand the difference between a merely weak signal and a really difficult communication that is easy to use in the field.
