[P1] FR12-01 - Inventory of urban trees after a heat wave Following an exceptionally hot period, the Green Spaces Department of a medium-sized city launched a targeted tree inventory along three very mineral-rich avenues. The aim was not to classify the entire tree population, but to quickly identify subjects who showed signs of stress and compare their condition with available maintenance information. The officers used a common chart to ensure that observations were comparable from one neighbourhood to another For each tree, the chart indicated the species, the approximate diameter of the trunk, the width of the planting pit and the visual condition of the foliage A separate category reported broken branches, recent injuries and signs of soil settlement The teams also noted the presence of waterproof coating within 50 cm of the trunk. Sampling began early in the morning, as a midday inspection would have been less comfortable and could have prompted officers to speed up the observations of symptoms to decision on 186 trees examined. Thirty-two had significantly more leaf loss than other subjects of the same species. This figure has not been interpreted as a mortality rate. Several trees had already undergone road works or had very small pits. Irrigation records showed that two streets had received irregular supplies during a week of restrictions. A programme of intervention was organised over four weeks. The young trees were deeply watered at defined intervals, while some of the stems were protected from stomping. Where possible, a small waterproof cover was removed around the pits to facilitate water infiltration. Officers avoided the harsh pruning, which was deemed unnecessary on already stressed trees. A further visit was scheduled for the end of September to check the development of foliage and the condition of the branches. The inventory has mainly improved the quality of decisions. Instead of treating all trees in the same way, the department now had dated observations, photographs and a maintenance history. The data did not replace the expertise of an arborist for complex cases, but it made it easier to prioritize sightings and communicate with tree dressers who signalled a tree in distress
============================================================

