[P1] FR12-03 - Audit of the cold chain in a dairy cooperative A dairy cooperative which collects milk from several farms carried out an in-house audit of its cold chain before the hottest time of year. The data showed that the majority of journeys remained within the expected range, but one Tuesday morning showed a gradual increase over the last 40 minutes The team first checked whether the sensor had been misplaced After confirming its position, they inspected the tank and discovered that a single worn-out trap joint had been replaced before the next trip The driver therefore requested that this sequence be kept within the normal range to indicate how an apparent instrumental anomaly could lead to a secondary check of the temperature of the tank. The secondary inspector was also concerned that the temperature of the tank did not exceed 15 degrees Fahrenheit, however, and that it was not useful to rest for more than two days. The driver was not immediately informed of the delay, but the secondary inspector did not have to rest for more than 48 hours. It's about the combination of time. temperature and condition of the equipment Checks at reception At the collection centre, the portable probes used for spot checks were compared with a reference Two devices had a gradient above O 6 °C and were removed until recalibration The operators also revised the method of cleaning the probes between batches to avoid turning a measuring procedure into a source of contamination. A plastic sheet was placed near the receiving station to remind the correct sequence at the end of the audit. The co-operative did not conclude that its cold chain was stable. Rather, it identified measurable checkpoints for joint width, probe reliability, travel time and reaction to delays. A second, shorter audit was scheduled six weeks later to verify that the actions decided were actually being implemented in the field.
